WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:02.350
Two last things about charts.

2
00:00:02.600 --> 00:00:07.200
First, read every note printed on a chart before you navigate with it.

3
00:00:07.450 --> 00:00:13.750
The notes cover regulations, cautions about shoaling or unreliable aids, and the datums.

4
00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:17.150
They’re the fine print, and they’re there for a reason.

5
00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:21.000
Second, electronic charts, which come in two kinds.

6
00:00:21.250 --> 00:00:24.925
Raster charts are scanned pictures of the paper chart.

7
00:00:25.175 --> 00:00:27.250
They look exactly like it.

8
00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:34.600
Electronic navigational charts, or E N Cs, are vector data that your plotter draws for itself.

9
00:00:34.850 --> 00:00:37.600
They’re powerful, but they have one trap.

10
00:00:37.850 --> 00:00:41.000
When you zoom out, what happens to the detail?

11
00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:43.750
It disappears.

12
00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:46.425
A rock can vanish from the screen.

13
00:00:46.675 --> 00:00:50.075
So zoom in along your whole route before you run it.

14
00:00:50.325 --> 00:00:54.200
And whichever kind you use, make sure it’s the current edition.

15
00:00:54.450 --> 00:00:56.350
That’s lesson two.

16
00:00:56.600 --> 00:00:58.375
Blue is shallow.

17
00:00:58.625 --> 00:01:00.475
Magenta matters.

18
00:01:00.725 --> 00:01:03.100
Treat doubtful hazards as real.

19
00:01:03.350 --> 00:01:05.000
And zoom in.

20
00:01:05.250 --> 00:01:07.375
Next time: buoys.
